  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14226204/assets/f6cac98f2c8c7cf182ef5cd88019c12a8b8c6ad8e52d0a01f8174332b366eb7e.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The upper half of our polar curve can be expressed in Cartesian coordinates as $y=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3.$ Write an integral expression that gives the exact area $A$ of the region enclosed between the curve and the  $x$-axis, as shown above.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A={\\int }_{a}^{b}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x,\\,\\,$ where  \n$a\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ and $\\,b\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}",
  "worked_solution": "We have the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\n$$\n\nand the limits of integration $x=-3$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, the required area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}y(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}}+3\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
